BODY = '''<h2>The 1:12 Rule from the ADA Standards</h2>
<p>The 2010 ADA Standards, published by the U.S. Access Board, say a ramp run should have a running slope not steeper than 1:12, meaning 1 inch of rise for every 12 inches of ramp length. They allow steeper slopes up to 1:8 in existing sites where space is limited, but only for small rises: 6 inches at slopes steeper than 1:12 up to 1:10, and 3 inches at slopes steeper than 1:10 up to 1:8. They prohibit anything steeper than 1:8.</p>
<p>The Standards also give a clear width of 36 inches for a ramp run, a maximum rise of 30 inches per run, and landings 60 inches long at the top and bottom. For a rise over 6 inches, they ask for handrails, and they ask for edge protection on both sides. The ADA covers public and commercial buildings and does not bind a private home, but it is the clearest published rule for how steep a ramp should be.</p>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, and a ramp that is too steep, too narrow or not secure is a hazard.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Measure the rise, then multiply by 12.</strong> A 2-inch step needs a 24-inch ramp, a 3-inch step 36 inches, a 6-inch step 72 inches and an 8-inch step 96 inches. The listings say their ramps work at much steeper slopes. For example, the VNN 3-foot ramp lists 3 to 9 inches, which is as steep as 1:4, so use the ADA rule to choose the length and not the listing's range.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Wheelchair Ramp</h2>
<h3>Measure the rise</h3>
<p>Measure from the ground to the top of the step or threshold in inches. Multiply by 12 to get the minimum ramp length in inches, and divide by 12 for feet.</p>
<h3>Length</h3>
<p>The picks are 24, 36, 72 and 96 inches long. At 1:12 they suit rises of 2, 3, 6 and 8 inches, so a 4-inch step needs at least a 4-foot ramp.</p>
<h3>Width and wheelbase</h3>
<p>The ramps are about 29 inches wide, narrower than the ADA's 36-inch clear width. The FACHNUO listing says the wheelbase should not exceed 27 inches. Measure your chair or scooter before you buy.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>Four listings give 800 pounds. The WHALEMOTOR title says 800 while its bullets say 606, so plan on 606. Add the weight of the person, the chair and anything carried.</p>
<h3>Weight of the ramp</h3>
<p>The ramps weigh from 10.9 to 49 pounds. A ramp you move daily should be light. One that stays in place can be heavier.</p>
<h3>Edges and handrails</h3>
<p>The ADA asks for handrails on ramps with a rise over 6 inches. The listings describe side baffles and reflective strips, not handrails. For a high step, add a rail or choose a different solution.</p>

<h2>Wheelchair Ramp Questions</h2>
<h3>How long a ramp do I need?</h3>
<p>Twelve inches of ramp for every inch of rise, according to the ADA Standards. A 3-inch step needs 36 inches, and a 6-inch step needs 72.</p>
<h3>Can I use a steeper ramp?</h3>
<p>The ADA allows slopes up to 1:8 in existing sites, but only for rises of 3 to 6 inches, and prohibits anything steeper. A steeper ramp is harder to push up and easier to tip on.</p>
<h3>Are portable ramps wide enough?</h3>
<p>The ramps here are about 29 inches wide, less than the ADA's 36 inches for a ramp run. Check your chair's wheelbase and the maker's width limit.</p>
<h3>Do I need handrails?</h3>
<p>The ADA asks for handrails on ramp runs with a rise of more than 6 inches. These portable ramps have side baffles, not handrails.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The VNN 3-foot ramp at $115.99 is the best pick for the most common case, a 3-inch step, with 2,231 reviews averaging 4.4 stars. The FACHNUO 2-foot ramp at $86.98 is the choice for a 2-inch threshold and a light carry, and the VEVOR at $73.63 is the cheapest 3-foot option, with a lower rating from far fewer reviews.</p>
<p>For a 6-inch porch step, the VNN 6-foot ramp at $249.99 gives the right length for 1:12, at 35.5 pounds. For an 8-inch step, the WHALEMOTOR 8-foot at $349.99 does, but its listing disagrees on capacity, so plan on 606 pounds.</p>
<p>Whichever you choose, measure first and match the length to the rise, and do not rely on the highest step height the listing claims.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The VNN 3-foot ramp is the best all-round portable ramp for a 3-inch step. Choose the FACHNUO 2-foot for a 2-inch threshold, the VNN 6-foot for a 6-inch step and the WHALEMOTOR for an 8-inch step. Measure the rise, multiply by 12, check your chair's width and ask a physical therapist or occupational therapist if the push up the ramp is a concern. See also our guides to <a href="/best-transport-wheelchairs/">transport wheelchairs</a> and <a href="/best-mobility-scooters/">mobility scooters</a>.</p>'''

